{"data":{"attachmentkeynames":["rs"],"desc":"Starts (or restarts) the PLAN WORKER: kb.plan autonomy on its own thread, independent of the executive's drive. PLAN_AUTONOMY (persisted agent setting, read live) is the only switch. Armed, it steps the next eligible item via agent.plan.step_item and goes straight on to the next - in-progress before accepted, then board order - so the LLM's latency is the only pacing. With nothing eligible it makes NO LLM calls: it checks the kb.plan facet hash every 5s. Eligible = accepted|in-progress, under MAX_PLAN_STEPS, not parked. PARENT COORDINATION IS THE DEFAULT (2026-11): a parent with proposed, blocked, or newly completed children is itself eligible for a coordination turn. It promotes the next proposed child (or re-accepts a blocked child only with a written remedy), then yields IN_PROGRESS. While any child is accepted/in-progress, that child executes FIRST and the parent waits in worker_status.waiting_for_children; the parent does not spend repeated turns or its step budget confirming an already-active child. When the child completes or blocks, the parent becomes eligible to coordinate the next transition. After all children finish, the parent gets a separate closing verified-DONE turn; there is no automatic parent rollup. Items in the blocked lifecycle are never globally auto-stepped. Failure guard: step_failed retries after 5m doubling to 1h, start_failed after 30s, anything else parks until a human changes that entry; toggling autonomy off/on clears parking. HUMAN NOTES count as a change and can un-park an item. PLAN_STEPS_PER_HOUR caps reasoning turns per sliding hour (unset/0 = unlimited). One step at a time, idempotent restart via a generation counter, fired at boot by worker_boot. Status: agent.plan.worker_status.","import":"use ndata::dataobject::DataObject;\nuse ndata::dataarray::DataArray;\nuse flowlang::datastore::DataStore;\nuse flowlang::command::Command;\nuse flowlang::flowlang::system::time::time;\nuse std::collections::HashMap;\n\n// Pure parent-scheduling rules at file scope so the worker and its regression\n// exercise exactly the same code. Command-body helpers are function-local in\n// generated Rust and cannot be imported by a fixture.\npub fn parent_is_coordination_task(claim: &str, entries: &Vec<DataObject>) -> bool {\n    entries.iter().any(|c| {\n        if c.try_get_string(\"parent\").unwrap_or_default() != claim { return false; }\n        !c.try_get_string(\"tags\").unwrap_or_default().to_lowercase()\n            .split(',').map(|t| t.trim()).any(|t| t == \"abandoned\")\n    })\n}\n\npub fn parent_waits_for_active_child(claim: &str, entries: &Vec<DataObject>) -> bool {\n    entries.iter().any(|c| {\n        if c.try_get_string(\"parent\").unwrap_or_default() != claim { return false; }\n        c.try_get_string(\"tags\").unwrap_or_default().to_lowercase()\n            .split(',').map(|t| t.trim())\n            .any(|t| t == \"accepted\" || t == \"in-progress\")\n    })\n}","params":[],"returntype":"JSONObject","type":"rust"},"id":"houmzl1a164f67b76ncc0","readers":[],"time":1792922825838,"username":"system","writers":[]}